Step 7 Socratic Question: What should I check to avoid the mistake of circular logic, which is drawing conclusions in advance into assumptions? Pause and observe the step carefully: pages 17 to 19. Since the four corners of the right triangle are SAS congruent and the sum of the two acute angles is 90 degrees, one interior angle of the center square is 180 degrees minus 90 degrees = 90 degrees, making it a square. If you organize the equation for the total area, the principle of a squared plus b squared = c squared is completed. What you must remember at this time is that the center square can be a rhombus just because the four sides have the same length c, so it is perfect to prove that the interior angle is 90 degrees (square) by using the sum of the two acute angles to be 90 degrees!

